Say whether the configuration thread exists at all

Run 456 put the match-path probe on the panel for the first time, and the
panel answered a question that had been open since run 453.  It showed

    nio: CFG enq D22AP out=4

and then nothing at all.  No "CFG wait", no "CFG pick", no "DSMi", no
"cand".  The probe of run 455 is only silent on the asynchronous path, and
the root nub is matched asynchronously, so the line that never appeared is
the one that says the configuration thread ran at all.

out=4 is the other half of the answer.  gOutstandingJobs is the number of
jobs that have been queued and not yet taken by a configuration thread, and
it was four when the root nub's job was queued.  So three jobs were already
waiting, and none of them had been picked up either.  The beacon on the
panel was still counting (V322, V323, ...), so the kernel was alive and
other threads were running: this is not a kernel that stopped, it is a
configuration thread that never existed or never got to run.

This patch turns that into an answer instead of an inference.  There are
three places where the thread can fail to come up, and all three are
silent today: configThread() is a do/while(false) loop, so "continue"
after a failed allocation, a failed init or a failed kernel_thread_start
leaves the function with nothing on the panel and no thread behind.  Each
one now says which it was:

    nio: CFG mk fail new         the _IOConfigThread object could not be
                                 allocated
    nio: CFG mk fail init        inst->init() failed
    nio: CFG mk fail start kr=N  kernel_thread_start failed, with the code
    nio: CFG mk ok t=...         the thread was created

and the thread itself says so on its way in, before it waits on the
semaphore:

    nio: CFG alive

The last one is the line that separates "never created" from "created but
never scheduled".  A "CFG mk ok" without a "CFG alive" means the thread
exists and does not run; a "CFG alive" without a "CFG pick" means it runs
and is stuck before it takes the job.

The enqueue line carries the two counters as well, because they are what
pingConfig compares to decide whether a thread is needed at all:

    nio: CFG enq <nub> out=N wt=N ct=N

wt is gNumWaitingThreads and ct is gNumConfigThreads.  The create decision
is gOutstandingJobs > wt, so if wt is larger than the number of threads
that really exist, no thread is ever created and every job waits for ever.
That is the one case where the queue would grow without a single failed
call, and it is the case these two numbers rule out.

Nothing here changes what the kernel does.  Every addition is a printf on
a path that already runs.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
index abd6360..59b01d2 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
@@ -5412,15 +5412,20 @@ _IOConfigThread::configThread( const char * name )
 
 	do {
 		if (!(inst = new _IOConfigThread)) {
+			printf("\nnio: CFG mk fail new");
 			continue;
 		}
 		if (!inst->init()) {
+			printf("\nnio: CFG mk fail init");
 			continue;
 		}
 		thread_t thread;
-		if (KERN_SUCCESS != kernel_thread_start(&_IOConfigThread::main, inst, &thread)) {
+		kern_return_t nio_kr = kernel_thread_start(&_IOConfigThread::main, inst, &thread);
+		if (KERN_SUCCESS != nio_kr) {
+			printf("\nnio: CFG mk fail start kr=%d", (int) nio_kr);
 			continue;
 		}
+		printf("\nnio: CFG mk ok t=%p", (void *) thread);
 
 		char threadName[MAXTHREADNAMESIZE];
 		snprintf(threadName, sizeof(threadName), "IOConfigThread_'%s'", name);
@@ -6114,6 +6119,7 @@ _IOConfigThread::main(void * arg, wait_result_t result)
 	_IOServiceJob * job;
 	IOService   *   nub;
 	bool            alive = true;
+	printf("\nnio: CFG alive");
 	kern_return_t   kr;
 	thread_precedence_policy_data_t precedence = { -1 };
 
@@ -6242,7 +6248,9 @@ _IOServiceJob::pingConfig( _IOServiceJob * job )
 	IOTakeLock( gJobsLock );
 
 	gOutstandingJobs++;
-	printf("\nnio: CFG enq %s out=%d", nub->getName(), (int) gOutstandingJobs);
+	printf("\nnio: CFG enq %s out=%d wt=%d ct=%d",
+	    nub->getName() ? nub->getName() : "?", (int) gOutstandingJobs,
+	    (int) gNumWaitingThreads, (int) gNumConfigThreads);
 	if (nub == gIOResources) {
 		gJobs->setFirstObject( job );
 	} else {
